%description:

The SINR-to-error mapping, from both ends: the BLER curve lookup
(PhyPisaData::getBler) and the CQI the feedback computation derives from it
(LteFeedbackComputationRealistic::getCqi). These are the two halves of one
round trip -- a CQI is chosen as the modulation whose BLER is closest to the
target, and the error model then asks what BLER that modulation actually has
-- and neither half is covered today.

No reference formula exists to grade this against: the curves are a table of
measured points (BLERvsSINR_15CQI_TU.h), not a closed form, so what is
checked here are the properties the table and its lookup have to have to
mean anything, and the model's own stated contracts.

Deliberately included is one thing that is arguably a defect. The test
asserts the behaviour as it is today, so that a refactoring which changes it
fails a named check that says what changed, rather than only a fingerprint:
PhyPisaData's minSnr()/maxSnr() do not agree with the table they are
commented as coming from. The checks below locate the real saturation
points by probing rather than by trusting either the constants or the
table, and then state the disagreement.

The one property that ought to hold and does not -- BLER falling monotonically
with SINR -- is split out into BlerCurveMonotonicity.test, which records it as
an expected failure.

%includes:
#include "SchedulerTestUtil.h"
#include "simu5g/common/binder/Binder.h"
#include "simu5g/common/blerCurves/PhyPisaData.h"
#include "simu5g/stack/phy/feedback/LteFeedbackComputationRealistic.h"

%global:
using namespace simu5g;
using namespace simu5g::unittest;

// The CQI mapping is a protected seam of the feedback computation, exposed
// here the way lib/MacTestStubs.h exposes the MAC's: the class under test is
// the real one, only its access specifier is stepped around.
class ProbeFeedback : public LteFeedbackComputationRealistic
{
  public:
    using LteFeedbackComputationRealistic::LteFeedbackComputationRealistic;
    using LteFeedbackComputationRealistic::getCqi;
};

// Probe: the lowest integer SINR at which CQI `cqi` is not yet hopeless.
static int lowestSnrBelowTotalLoss(PhyPisaData& p, int cqi)
{
    for (int s = -60; s <= 60; s++)
        if (p.getBler(cqi, s) < 1.0)
            return s;
    return 61;
}

// Probe: the lowest integer SINR at which CQI `cqi` is error-free.
static int lowestErrorFreeSnr(PhyPisaData& p, int cqi)
{
    for (int s = -60; s <= 60; s++)
        if (p.getBler(cqi, s) == 0.0)
            return s;
    return 61;
}

%activity:
Checker c;
PhyPisaData pisa;

//
// --- the curve lookup: PhyPisaData::getBler ---
//
{
    // A block error rate is a probability. Nothing downstream range-checks it,
    // so the day an interpolation goes out of bounds it would silently become a
    // negative or >1 error probability in isReceptionSuccessful().
    bool inRange = true;
    for (int cqi = 1; cqi <= 15; cqi++)
        for (int s = -30; s <= 50; s++) {
            double b = pisa.getBler(cqi, s);
            if (!(b >= 0.0 && b <= 1.0))
                inRange = false;
        }
    c.check("every tabulated BLER is a probability in [0,1]", inRange);
}
{
    // A more aggressive modulation is never the safer one at the same SINR.
    // Without this the CQI scale would not be an ordering at all.
    bool ordered = true;
    for (int s = -30; s <= 50; s++)
        for (int cqi = 1; cqi < 15; cqi++)
            if (pisa.getBler(cqi + 1, s) < pisa.getBler(cqi, s))
                ordered = false;
    c.check("at a fixed SINR, BLER is non-decreasing in CQI", ordered);
}
{
    // The saturations of GetBLER_TU: total loss below the curve's first point,
    // error-free at and above its last.
    c.expectValue("CQI 1 is hopeless far below its curve", (long long)(pisa.getBler(1, -30) * 100), 100);
    c.expectValue("CQI 15 is error-free far above its curve", (long long)(pisa.getBler(15, 50) * 100), 0);
}
{
    // getBler's SINR parameter is an int, so the curve is only ever sampled at
    // 1 dB granularity even though GetBLER_TU interpolates on a double. A
    // refactoring that widens the parameter would start sampling between these
    // points, which is a behaviour change worth noticing: CQI 7's curve falls
    // from 0.036 at 10 dB to 0.008 at 11, so 10.9 dB would read differently.
    double between = 10.9;
    c.check("the lookup is sampled at integer SINR only",
            pisa.getBler(7, between) == pisa.getBler(7, 10));
}
{
    // blerShift displaces the curve along the SINR axis, and it does so once:
    // asking with a shift of k is asking k dB further along with no shift.
    PhyPisaData shifted;
    shifted.setBlerShift(3);
    bool equivalent = true;
    for (int cqi = 1; cqi <= 15; cqi++)
        for (int s = -20; s <= 40; s++)
            if (shifted.getBler(cqi, s) != pisa.getBler(cqi, s + 3))
                equivalent = false;
    c.check("blerShift is applied exactly once, as a shift of the SINR axis", equivalent);

    // ...and it moves the declared bounds with it, in the direction that keeps
    // the same stretch of curve addressable.
    c.expectValue("minSnr moves with blerShift", shifted.minSnr(), pisa.minSnr() - 3);
    c.expectValue("maxSnr moves with blerShift", shifted.maxSnr(), pisa.maxSnr() - 3);
}
{
    // Where the curves really start and stop, found by probing, against what
    // PhyPisaData declares. minSnr() agrees. maxSnr() does not: it claims 40
    // while the last CQI is already error-free at 38, so there is a 2 dB strip
    // in which getCqi does not take its CQI-15 shortcut and searches instead --
    // arriving at 15 anyway, because every CQI reports zero BLER there.
    //
    // The comments in PhyPisaData.h name SINR_15_CQI_TU[0][0] and [14][15] as
    // these two constants; neither matches (-14.5 and 38.0 respectively).
    // minSnr() lands right anyway, because CQI 1's first two tabulated points
    // are both BLER 1, so -14 interpolates to total loss just as -14.5 does.
    int firstUseful = lowestSnrBelowTotalLoss(pisa, 1);
    int firstErrorFree = lowestErrorFreeSnr(pisa, 15);
    c.expectValue("minSnr() is the last SINR at which even CQI 1 is hopeless",
            pisa.minSnr(), firstUseful - 1);
    c.expectValue("CQI 15 is error-free from 38 dB up", firstErrorFree, 38);
    c.check("maxSnr() overshoots the point where the last curve ends",
            pisa.maxSnr() > firstErrorFree,
            "maxSnr=" + std::to_string(pisa.maxSnr()) + " errorFreeFrom=" + std::to_string(firstErrorFree));
}

//
// --- the other half: the CQI derived from those curves ---
//
{
    // The feedback computation needs a Binder only to reach its PhyPisaData.
    Binder *binder = new Binder();
    ProbeFeedback fb(binder, 0.01, 1);

    // The scheduler acts on this number: more SINR must never yield a lower
    // CQI, or a UE that moves closer to the cell is served a slower modulation.
    bool monotone = true;
    for (double s = -20; s < 45; s += 1.0)
        if (fb.getCqi(s + 1) < fb.getCqi(s))
            monotone = false;
    c.check("CQI is non-decreasing in SINR", monotone);

    c.expectValue("CQI is 0 at the bottom of the usable range",
            fb.getCqi(pisa.minSnr()), 0);
    c.expectValue("CQI is 15 above the top of the usable range",
            fb.getCqi(pisa.maxSnr() + 1), 15);

    // getCqi ROUNDS its SINR (floor(snr+0.5)), while the error model TRUNCATES
    // the same quantity (StochasticChannelModel: int snr = snrV[band]). The two
    // therefore disagree by up to 1 dB about which curve point applies to the
    // same channel. Pinned as the model's current contract, so that a
    // refactoring which unifies them fails here and says so.
    c.expectValue("getCqi rounds a fractional SINR up from .5",
            fb.getCqi(9.5), fb.getCqi(10.0));
    c.expectValue("getCqi rounds a fractional SINR down below .5",
            fb.getCqi(9.49), fb.getCqi(9.0));

    delete binder;
}
{
    // A CQI is the modulation whose BLER is closest to the target, so tolerating
    // more errors can only ask for a faster one. This is the whole meaning of
    // the targetBler parameter, and nothing else in the suite exercises it.
    Binder *binder = new Binder();
    ProbeFeedback strict(binder, 0.001, 1);
    ProbeFeedback lax(binder, 0.1, 1);
    bool neverSlower = true;
    bool everFaster = false;
    for (double s = -10; s < 40; s += 1.0) {
        Cqi a = strict.getCqi(s);
        Cqi b = lax.getCqi(s);
        if (b < a)
            neverSlower = false;
        if (b > a)
            everFaster = true;
    }
    c.check("a laxer target BLER never asks for a slower modulation", neverSlower);
    c.check("a laxer target BLER does ask for a faster one somewhere", everFaster);
    delete binder;
}

c.summary();

%contains: stdout
modelchecks=18 modelfailures=0

%not-contains: stdout
FAILED
